@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* ConfirmButton's Overview, written by hand after Dialog's: confirming a form post and showing
   what's affected (previewed in each mode), the other things confirming can do (code), and its
   own words. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A confirm button is a button that asks before it acts, for actions that are hard to undo, such as deleting. It opens a small alert dialog, and only the dialog's confirm button carries the action out.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Inside a form, confirming submits the form, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too. The dialog sits beside the button, inside the form it submits.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>To ask a question from C# on interactive pages, use <Code>DialogService.ConfirmAsync</Code>; see <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog#dialogs-from-code">Dialog</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Confirming a form post</Heading>
<TextBlock>A form can have several confirm buttons, such as Archive and Delete, handled by one submit handler. To post which action was confirmed, set <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code> on each button. On interactive pages, set <Code>OnConfirm</Code> to run the same action.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="confirm-button-usage" />
<List>
    <ListItem>Don't use <Code>action</Code>, <Code>method</Code> or <Code>target</Code> as the <Code>Name</Code>. A control with one of those names hides the form's own property, and the form post goes to the wrong URL.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>An interactive page handles the form in C# without posting it, so nothing fills <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code> there. Give each button an <Code>OnConfirm</Code>, as this example does.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On static pages, the confirm button submits the form whether or not <Code>OnConfirm</Code> is set.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Showing what's affected</Heading>
<TextBlock>The dialog can show more than a one-line message, such as a list of what will be deleted. Put that content in <Code>Body</Code> instead of <Code>Message</Code>. To change the confirm button's words, set <Code>ConfirmText</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="confirm-button-affected" />
<TextBlock>Name the action on the confirm button ("Delete files", not "OK"), so the choice is clear on its own.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Other ways to confirm</Heading>
<TextBlock>Confirming can follow a link or submit a different form, instead of the form the button is in. To follow a link when confirmed, set <Code>Href</Code>; to submit a form elsewhere on the page, set <Code>Form</Code> to that form's id. To post the button's own form to another URL, set <Code>FormAction</Code>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Others</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Opening the dialog</Heading>
<TextBlock>The button opens its dialog with the browser's <Code>command</Code> attribute. Where the browser doesn't support it, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> opens the dialog instead. So in an older browser with JavaScript off, the dialog can't open: keep a way to act without it, such as a separate page for deleting.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, set <Code>Loading</Code> while <Code>OnConfirm</Code>'s work runs. The button can't open the dialog again until the work is done.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The dialog's buttons have text of their own. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>ConfirmText</Code></td><td>The confirm button's text</td><td>"Confirm"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>CancelText</Code></td><td>The cancel button's text</td><td>"Cancel"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Others = """
        @* Follow a link. *@
        <ConfirmButton Title="Sign out?" Href="/account/sign-out">Sign out</ConfirmButton>

        @* Submit a form elsewhere on the page. *@
        <ConfirmButton Title="Discard this draft?" Form="draft" Name="intent" Value="discard"
                       Accent="Accent.Danger">Discard</ConfirmButton>

        @* Post the form it's in to another address. *@
        <form method="post" action="/orders/1042">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <ConfirmButton Title="Cancel this order?" FormAction="/orders/1042/cancel"
                           Accent="Accent.Danger">Cancel order</ConfirmButton>
        </form>
        """;
}
